ners and your  dancing with the hopes that even if you  don't dance you can use these insights  to be a better leader at work the first  big takeaway is that you should use  energy wisely dance like most things in  life is an act of focused energy like  our friendly physicists to explain  energy cannot be created nor destroyed  only transferred or transformed the same  applies in leading and following whether  on the dance floor or in the conference  room we're always managing directing  absorbing and sharing energy even when  we're not aware that this is happening  perhaps this is why often unaware and  maybe more disconnected leaders  accidentally step on your toes they're  not aware of where their energy is going  let alone where it is in relation to  yours and before you know it goes our  big toe  so I think that leaders should manage  not just numbers and targets but also  energy let me illustrate this with an  example  I used to work at Google and at Google  unlike most companies engineers nominate  themselves for a promotion  the reason for this is that progress in  engineering can sometimes be hard to  spot externally so the onus is on the  individual to dominate him or herself  when he or she feels ready seems super  fair well the issues was that women were  not nominating themselves for promotion  at the same race that their male  colleagues were imagine you're a leader  at Google what do you do about this Alan  Eustace was a leader in the engineering  organization when he got this  information and he was really wise what  he did was he used his position to  strategically redirect the energy in the  organization he drafted an email where  he cited academic studies that show that  girls don't raise our hands as often in  the classroom and that women don't speak  up often in meetings and he said that  the same thing was happening with  promotions he sent us to every engineer  at Google and he told the women to  advocate for themselves you see in  realizing w